------------------------------------
STAGE 3: Changing libcosmic to egui:
------------------------------------

1) Just answer this questions without changing anything: What would be the pros/cons for changing libcosmic to egui?
 - Would the app be faster to launch? Would it be more lightweight on a very slow (rpi) computer?
 - Would it make the code less complicated? egui is immediate-mode and by default repaints every frame so we need to configure it to only repaint on input/events (ctx.request_repaint(), but this is the only complication compared to libcosmic (which is event based and not redrawing if nothing is pressed).
 - Could it still handle the fonts, color schemes, superscript/subscript alignment, transparency
 - How would be the RAM usage, because right now it uses ~130MB RAM which is a bit high for a small 20MB calculator. egui uses less memory and produces probably a smaller executable because less dependencies are pulled in. libcosmic: Elm-style architecture requires to define a Message enum for every event (digit presses, operators, clear, etc.), implement update() to mutate state per message, and a separate view() to build the widget tree from state, plus the cosmic::Application trait boilerplate (init, title, etc.). So probably we can get rid of all that with libcosmic.
 - Can we just skip GPU acceleration to have simpler codebase and faster startup and use egui with "tiny-skia" instead? I still want to use the KDE Plasma or Cosmic Desktop blur effect, but the compositor should do the GPU accelerated blur effect. I think egui needs to request this, it's probably not enough to just make the background transparent.

2) The startup time of the application could be optimized to be much faster. It's just a lightweight calculator so I want it to open instantly.
Ideas:
 - Maybe the font list loading slows it down as it needs to read through many external files. If it's something which is only visible in the settings then please make the app startup faster and grey out the settings option until their contents (e.g. fonts list) are loaded. Of course the app needs to load the font which is currently used, but that's the only font we need to launch the application quickly.
 - Does libcosmic add to the application startup delay? I assume egui without gpu acceleration would be much more lightweight and fast to startup, right? I want it to be super fast even on a RaspberryPi
 - The executable binary is around 20MB which is a lot for a small calculator. It probably has lots of bloat, so please get rid of the dead code and consider adding strip = true and lto = true and opt-level = "z" vs "3" and panic = "abort" and make sure we don't use UPX compression. You can evaluate these options and discover new ones as well to optimize for the fastest cold startup possible. Also check for enabled features you don't use — many crates have optional features (like full on tokio) that you can disable via default-features = false and picking only what you need.
 - Reduce dependencies where possible. E.g.: Switch to the musl target to get rid of glibc dependency. musl libc is statically linked, so there's no runtime dependency on the host's C library at all this way.
```rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl```
 - Big contributors to bloat are often:
  a) Async runtimes (tokio) — if you don't need async, drop it
  b) Regex, serde with derive, clap with all features — these can pull in a lot of generated code
  c) Run cargo bloat --release (install with cargo install cargo-bloat) to see exactly which crates/functions are eating the space

3) Implement negotiation of xdg-decoration protocol so KDE Kwin, Gnome Mutter, Xfce xfwl4 can use their own window decoration. We can maybe save this in the config.toml, so we don't need to run the negotiation every time we start the application. Do whatever is faster for the app startup. Maybe negotiation is needed every time we start, I don't know.


----------------------
STAGE 4: Fixing fonts:
----------------------

1) Some fonts which have italic effect by default on the regular weight (e.g. Monte Carlo or Vivaldi) will go out from the main display area on the right (the first character). It looks like the bottom of the font is correctly aligned but the top is not checked.

2) The vertical alignment of the fonts needs to be fixed as well. To make it work for most fonts we should align the characters to a middle line, because I want to see the numbers to be aligned vertically in the middle when they are in between brackets. We should not do it on a character basis, because I don't want "log" or "tan" to have misaligned letters, so come up with something clever here.

3) The horizontal font size scaling is not accurate. We should be able to use the available horizontal and vertical space efficiently regardless of which font is used. Right now if I paste in a really long number e.g.: "99999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999" it will be smaller than it should be and only using about 80% of the horizontal space. But the same thing happens if I enter 15 digits: "888888888888888", it never fills the screen to the right side, no matter how I resize the window. I think the problem might be that the extra vertical space is added above the last expression and therefore cannot be used by the main display which is below the last expression.

4) The "Recommended" text should be removed from the font list and the whole "recommended" font list and related code should be removed as well. Instead, we should write "Theme default" in the same place where "Recommended" was for the single font which is hardcoded for the currently selected theme.

5) If the font defined in the config.toml does not exist (because the user mistyped it when editing the config file, or just simply the default hardcoded font is not installed) then we choose the system's general font. The fonts should not apply to settings menu. The settings should always use the default system font.


--------------------
STAGE 5: Misc fixes:
--------------------

1) When the user pastes something from the clipboard check the contents before deciding how to paste it. If it has operators and other stuff then overwrite what is on the current display (which is the same as the current behavior). If it's a number (with or without a decimal dot or comma) then just paste it as an operand, so in this case if the user already types "52" then the pasted number should replace that, but if the user typed "52+" then it should come after the plus sign. So the last operand (empty or not) should be from the clipboard if its a number. If it's a too big number, then silently ignore the paste (I think this is the current behavior but test it).

2) When pasting expression from clipboard it should first convert it into ASCII compatible (with proper "-" symbol, etc, and it should apply the pretty print immediately, so the user don't even see the ascii expression (unless the ascii display toggle is on)

3) Add a toggle for scientific notation (default ON). Of course this needs to saved in config.toml as well.

4) Why the application does not appear in the applications list in KDE System monitor? I can only find it among the processes. Maybe because it was not installed as a normal package?

5) Add Translation support: Only for UI elements e.g.: "History", "Scientific mode", "Decimal Separator" and error messages e.g.: "Undefined: Negative number under even root". Autodetect system language on first startup and save it in config.toml. Add GUI option in settings to be able to change it.

6) When backspace removes the closing bracket it should remove the matching opening bracket as well

7) Wrong "-" character is used in the main display and last expression. It is okay to use regular "-" in ascii mode, but for pretty display we should use the same which is on the minus button face because the regular dash does not align well with the plus or divide symbols vertically

8) The toggle which is "show angle mode and memory buttons" should be split into two separate toggles: one for basic and one for scientific mode, so we can adjust it separately. By default it should be off for basic and on for scientific mode.

9) Automatically detect if the currently used font supports euler character or not. If not then use regular "e" in pretty print display. This is needed because fonts which have thin weight usually don't support this character and then it gets it from some default system font which will not be thin.

